WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  waving malian flags thousands gathered  in mali's capital bamako  to protest against the former colonial  power france  [Applause]  there was even a cardboard cut out of  french president emmanuel macron which  was later set on fire  [Music]  the crowd was celebrating the expulsion  of the french ambassador  he was given 72 hours to leave after the  french foreign minister described mali's  military leaders as illegitimate and out  of control  there are thousands and thousands and  thousands of malians today who say no to  france so what the european union and  france needs to do is respect the malian  authorities they need to understand that  the authorities in charge today are the  only ones who can speak for our country  the rally was called by prominent  nationalist activist and member of the  transitional authority adama bendyara  who the european union says helped  overthrow mali's government in august  2020.  the demonstration happening on the same  day he was sanctioned by the eu for his  role in the coup  the measures include travel bans and  asset freezes and target five people  including the transitional prime  minister cho'gal maeger and members of  interim president asimi goyta's inner  circle  relations with the eu have soured since  goiter put himself in charge and reneged  on a promise to hold elections this  month the european sanctions are a sign  of rationing up the pressure but simply  follow what the africans have done  themselves i think dialogue certainly is  still an option and that's the preferred  course of action by both ecowas and the  international community but diara  described the sanctions as an honour  saying expelling the french ambassador  is only the latest step in getting rid  of paris's influence  it's an important step in the fight but  the victory must go all the way  the next step must be the departure of  french forces and then we will start to  move towards economic and monetary  sovereignty  there are about 5 000 foreign troops in  mali deployed to contain years of  insecurity in the north  they include missions from france the  wider eu and the united nations  we don't want any foreign troops in mali  they've been here for eight years now  and nothing has changed  there were russian flags in the crowd at  friday's rally russian mercenaries were  welcomed by mali's military leaders last  year and diara says he will rely on  moscow to ensure france is forced out  for good  alexia o'brien al jazeera

